As is common in Oceanic linguistics, the term 'labiovelar' encompasses two classes of [[consonant]]s: six [[velarized]] bilabial occlusives {{IPA|/ᵐbʷ, pᵐʷ, pʷ, (pʷʰ),{{clarify|date=May 2026|reason=Marginal? Paper clearly puts in parens, but can't find an explanation for why}} mʷ, m̥ʷ/}}; and four [[labial–velar]] approximants {{IPA|{{IPAplink|w}}, {{IPAplink|w̥}}, {{IPAplink|w̃}}, w̥̃/}}.

The phoneme {{IPA|/s/}} possibly corresponds to an [[Phonological change|etymological]] *{{IPAslink|cʰ}}, a [[phonological gap]].

Some speakers have a simplified inventory of consonants in medial position, with oral occlusives and voiceless continuants generally merging into voiced continuants and (in the case of alveolars) the vibrant, and postnasalized and voiceless nasal occlusives into voiced nasals, with the exception of [[loanword]]s. For those speakers, a similar allophonic [[free variation]] can also produce {{IPA|[k~ɣ]}}, {{IPA|[t~r]}}, and {{IPA|[tʰ~r̥]}} in initial positions in less careful speech.

In final position, only eight phonemes contrast: {{IPA|/m n ɲ ŋ p t c k/}}.

